<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Global Routing Decision</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: The first step involves determining the appropriate expert module based on input complexity through a global router. The global router </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> analyzes the input feature </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{din}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{din}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and produces routing probabilities for both shallow and deep expert modules. This binary decision mechanism is trained using reinforcement learning with combined time and accuracy rewards to automatically balance computational efficiency and task performance. The router learns to assign simple tasks to shallow experts for faster inference while directing complex tasks to deep experts for higher accuracy. The calculation process is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g}=\text{Softmax}(R_{g}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g}=\text{Softmax}(R_{g}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\arg\max(P_{g})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\arg\max(P_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Local Expert Selection via Top-k Gating</span><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: Within the selected expert module (shallow or deep), a local router determines which specific experts to activate using a top-k gating mechanism. The local router </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> computes attention weights across all 8 experts within the chosen module, where each expert specializes in different aspects of the feature space. The top-k selection ensures load balancing while maintaining computational efficiency, as only the two most relevant experts are activated for each input. This sparse activation reduces computational overhead compared to dense expert utilization while preserving model expressiveness. The calculation process is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}=\text{Softmax}(R_{s}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}=\text{Softmax}(R_{s}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{i_{1},i_{2}}=\text{top-k}(p_{s},k=2)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">top-k</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{i_{1},i_{2}}=\text{top-k}(p_{s},k=2)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Forward Computation Through Expert Architectures</span><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: The core computation differs significantly between shallow and deep expert modules to accommodate varying task complexities. Shallow experts employ single-layer MLPs with weight matrices </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{s}^{(k)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{hidden}\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{s}^{(k)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{hidden}\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for rapid feature transformation, suitable for tasks requiring quick responses. Deep experts utilize three-layer architectures where the first layer is copied from pre-trained shallow experts and frozen during training, while the additional layers </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{d}^{(k,2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{d}^{(k,2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{d}^{(k,3)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{hidden}\times d_{hidden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{d}^{(k,3)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{hidden}\times d_{hidden}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> are trained on complex tasks. Residual connections between the first and third layers facilitate gradient flow and enhance representational capacity. The calculation process is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{d}^{(k,3)}=\sigma(W_{d}^{(k,3)}h_{d}^{(k,2)}+b_{d}^{(k,3)})+h_{d}^{(k,1)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{d}^{(k,3)}=\sigma(W_{d}^{(k,3)}h_{d}^{(k,2)}+b_{d}^{(k,3)})+h_{d}^{(k,1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Weighted Combination and Output Projection</span><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: The final step aggregates outputs from the selected top-k experts using their respective routing probabilities as weights, followed by a learned projection to the desired output dimensionality. The weighted combination leverages the routing probabilities </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> or </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> computed during local expert selection to create a smooth interpolation between expert predictions. The output projection matrices </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{out}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{out}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{out}^{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{hidden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{out}^{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{hidden}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> are module-specific, allowing for different output transformations optimized for shallow versus deep expert representations. This design ensures that the final output </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> effectively combines expert knowledge while maintaining the desired output format. The calculation process is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=W_{out}\left(\sum_{k\in top-k,k=2}p^{(k)}h^{(k)}\right)+b_{out}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=W_{out}\left(\sum_{k\in top-k,k=2}p^{(k)}h^{(k)}\right)+b_{out}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Lightweight Evaluator</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We have constructed a lightweight evaluation module. This module adopts a two-layer multi-layer perceptron architecture with a hidden layer dimension of </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. The evaluator takes the internally generated attention weight distribution of the model as input, aiming to map it onto a two-dimensional metric space. This yields two outputs: the first is </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, quantifying the contribution of this attention pattern to the model’s final prediction; the second is </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, assessing the intrinsic structural characteristics of the attention distribution. The calculation formula is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{i},C_{i}]^{T}=W_{2}\cdot\text{ReLU}(W_{1}\cdot\text{AttnWeights}(x_{i})+b_{1})+b_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">ReLU</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">AttnWeights</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{i},C_{i}]^{T}=W_{2}\cdot\text{ReLU}(W_{1}\cdot\text{AttnWeights}(x_{i})+b_{1})+b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">here, </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AttnWeights(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AttnWeights(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> denotes the attention weight vector generated by the attention mechanism within the model after processing the input </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Taking the sentiment analysis in </span><a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Dynamic Adaptive Shared Experts with Grouped Multi-Head Attention Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> as an example, the </span><math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">=0.82 of ’pretty’ triggers 3 experts, while the </span><math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">=0.11 of ’the’ only activates 1 expert, which verifies the effectiveness of our evaluator.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">For our experimental validation, we incorporate the proposed DASG-MoE framework within Switch Transformer, a leading Mixture-of-Experts Transformer architecture that functions as our comparative baseline. Our comprehensive evaluation methodology encompasses three distinct experimental phases: (1) initial pre-training assessment utilizing 5T tokens obtained from the Internet, (2) subsequent fine-tuning validation employing pre-trained model configurations on the C4 (Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus) dataset</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a><span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, and (3) comprehensive token significance analysis</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a><span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> spanning diverse natural language processing applications.
To ensure robust performance assessment across multiple NLP domains following fine-tuning procedures, we employ the General Language Understanding Evaluation (GLUE) benchmark according to its established protocols</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a><span id="S5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Our experimental infrastructure consists of 8 NVIDIA A100 GPU units, providing the computational resources necessary for thorough model evaluation and comparison.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The methodological framework and experimental parameters are comprehensively documented below.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Benchmarks</span><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: MiniCPM4 is primarily pre-trained using Chinese and English corpora. Therefore, we selected the following datasets to evaluate model performance, specifically the knowledge-intensive evaluation set MMLU</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, CMMLU</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, and CEval</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, which are used for English and Chinese evaluations, respectively. Additionally, we include reasoning evaluation datasets, including the general reasoning dataset BigBench Hard (BBH)</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, mathematical reasoning GSM8K</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and MATH500 </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, as well as code reasoning MBPP</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and HumanEval </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Baseline Models</span><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: We compared DASG-MoE-Instruct against several widely adopted open-source large language models, including Qwen3-8B</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, GLM4-9B</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Gemma3-12B</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, LLaMA3.1-8B</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, and Phi4-14B</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. The implementation results are shown in </span><a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experimental Analysis ‣ Dynamic Adaptive Shared Experts with Grouped Multi-Head Attention Mixture of Experts" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Among these, DASG-MoE-Instruct is obtained by fine-tuning the DASG-MoE model with instructions.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Pre-training Settings</span><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: The pre-training process for the DASG-MoE and Switch Transformer models utilized 5T tokens obtained from the Internet. The model architecture employs an expert configuration, including 8, 16, and 32 units</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, with shallow modules containing 8 experts and deep modules containing 8 experts also included in the calculation</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. It features 768 dimensional token embeddings, 3072 dimensional hidden layer processing, and 12 attention heads.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning Settings</span><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: Our experimental setup is based on the pre-trained ST-Base model parameters, which uses 16 units configured by experts. In order to establish the DASG-MoE and ST architectures for subsequent fine-tuning processes, we replaced the original experts with 8 deep experts and 8 shallow experts, enabling comprehensive evaluation on the GLUE benchmark suite.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The General Language Understanding Evaluation benchmark encompasses diverse natural language processing challenges organized into distinct categories. Single-sentence classification encompasses SST-2</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, which performs binary sentiment analysis on movie review data to determine positive or negative polarity, alongside CoLA</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, designed for assessing linguistic acceptability and grammatical comprehension.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Similarity and paraphrase identification tasks include MRPC</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Microsoft Research Paraphrase Corpus) for detecting semantic equivalence, and QQP (Quora Question Pairs) for identifying duplicate question instances. Additionally, the benchmark incorporates four natural language inference challenges: MNLI</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Multi-Sentence Natural Language Inference) for cross-genre textual entailment, QNLI</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Question Natural Language Inference) for question-answering inference, RTE</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.I1.i4.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Recognizing Textual Entailment) for premise-hypothesis relationships, and WNLI (Winograd Natural Language Inference) for pronoun resolution tasks.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
